faq:
  - q: "What should a LinkedIn connection message say?"
    a: "Keep it under 300 characters (the character limit for connection request notes). Reference something specific — a shared connection, a post they wrote, their role, a company you both know. State who you are in one line. Do not ask for a job or a referral in the first message. Examples: 'Hi [Name], I'm a senior PM moving from B2B SaaS to fintech. Noticed your work at [Company] on [area] — would love to connect.' / 'Hi [Name], came across your post on [topic] — strong take. I'm a staff engineer exploring opportunities in distributed systems. Happy to connect.'"
  - q: "Should I always include a note with a LinkedIn connection request?"
    a: "Not always — a note is valuable when you have something specific to say. A generic note ('I'd like to add you to my professional network') is worse than no note. Send a note when: you have a specific mutual connection to mention, you have read their content and have a genuine comment, you are reaching out specifically about their company or role. Skip the note for generic network-building with people who may recognize your profile."
  - q: "How do I message a recruiter on LinkedIn?"
    a: "Keep it brief, specific, and value-forward. State your target role, your current level, and why you are reaching out. Example: 'Hi [Name], I'm a senior software engineer with 8 years in distributed systems and platform infrastructure — recently shipped [specific outcome]. Saw you work with companies in [space] and wanted to reach out. Happy to share more if there's a fit.'"
  - q: "How do I message someone asking for a referral?"
    a: "Not in the first message. Build rapport first: connect, comment on their content, engage briefly. Then, in a follow-up message after connection: 'I'm applying to [role] at [Company] and noticed you work there. I've been following [specific project/company news] — would love your perspective on the team/culture. No pressure at all if timing is off.'"
